      "before": "  // Handle events\n  $module.addEventListener('click', this.handleClick.bind(this))",
      "after": "  // Handle events\n  $module.addEventListener('click', this.handleClick.bind(this))\n  $module.addEventListener('change', this.handleClick.bind(this))"
    }
  ],
  "expected_accessibility_effect": "Keyboard users can navigate among radio options using arrow keys and see dependent fields appear/disappear correctly, satisfying WCAG 2.1.1 Keyboard, 2.4.3 Focus Order, and 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value.",
  "functional_risks": [
    "Both click and change events may fire for the same interaction, but setAttributes is idempotent so impact is minimal.",
    "Ensuring the change listener does not affect non-radio elements within the module."
  ],
  "verification_requirements": [
    "Verify that arrow key navigation between email, text, and none options correctly shows/hides the email address and mobile number fields.",
    "Verify that clicking radios with mouse continues to work as before.",
    "Verify that the form's reflected value matches the selected radio after keyboard interaction.",
    "Ensure no regression in existing automated tests for the radios component."
  ]
}

reasoning:
We need to analyze the problem.

The test scenario:

Goal: Arrow down from "Text message" option (#reminder-2) to the opt-out answer (#reminder-none) should hide the mobile number field (#conditional-reminder-2).

Observed failure: At step index=2 (the second arrowdown press), after moving focus to #reminder-none, assertion expects visible selector "#conditional-reminder-2" false but it's true (still visible). So when selecting none, the conditional for text message remains shown incorrectly.

Looking at the UI structure: There are two groups of radios? Actually first group includes email, text, etc., each having conditionals. Then a divider "or". Then another group for opt-out.

In the provided HTML:
```
<div class="govuk-radios govuk-radios--conditional" data-module="govuk-radios" id="channel-radios">
  ... email ...
  ... conditional-reminder (email)
  ... text message ...
  ... conditional-reminder-2 (mobile)
</div>
<div class="govuk-radios__divider">or</div>
<div class="govuk-radios" data-module="govuk-radios" id="optout-radios">
  <div class="govuk-radios__item">
    <input class="govuk-radios__input" id="reminder-none" name="reminder" type="radio" value="none">
    <label class="govuk-label govuk-radios__label" for="reminder-none">I do not want reminders</label>
  </div>
</div>
```

Thus there are two separate divs both with data-module="govuk-radios". Each gets instantiated as a Radios component via script: document.querySelectorAll('[data-module="govuk-radios"]').forEach(...). So there are two independent Radios instances: one for channel-radios (email/text) and one for optout-radios (none). The issue likely arises because when focusing on the opt-out radio, the Radios instance for channel-radios does not get notified that a different radio (with same name "reminder") was selected, so it doesn't update its conditional visibility based on the checked state of its own radios.

Specifically, the Radios component sets aria-expanded and toggles conditional hidden based on the checked state of its own inputs when init runs (setting attributes based on initial checked state) and on click events (handleClick). However, keyboard navigation changes focus but does not change the checked state until you press Space or Enter? Actually for radio buttons, using arrow keys changes the checked state automatically (as per HTML spec). Indeed, when you arrow down from a radio to another radio in the same radiogroup, the newly focused radio becomes checked. But here we have two separate radiogroups? They share the same name attribute "reminder". All radios have name="reminder". So they are actually part of the same logical radiogroup despite being in separate divs. However, the Radios component only looks at inputs within its own $module (the div). So when the optout radio gets checked via arrow key, the channel-radios Radios instance does not get a click event (since it's not a click) and does not run its setAttributes to update aria-expanded and toggle conditional visibility. Therefore the conditional for text message remains visible (since its associated radio #reminder-2 is still considered not checked? Actually after arrow down to none, #reminder-2 becomes unchecked, but the channel Radios instance hasn't updated its state, so it still thinks #reminder-2 is checked? Let's see: In init, Radios.prototype.setAttributes is called for each input that has aria-controls, based on inputIsChecked = $input.checked at init time. Initially, which radio is checked? Probably none? Let's check observed state before first action: The journey start: seek #reminder (maybe first radio email?). Actually first action: seek #reminder (id="reminder"? Wait there is an input with id="reminder" for email? Yes first radio email has id="reminder". So they seek to that. Then ArrowDown moves to #reminder-2 (text). Then ArrowDown moves to #reminder-none (opt-out). After that, the observed focus is on #reminder-none, and they assert that #conditional-reminder-2 should be hidden (not visible). But it's still visible, meaning the conditional for text message is still shown.

Thus the bug: The Radios component does not respond to change events (like when a radio's checked state changes via keyboard). It only responds to click events. So we need to also listen for change events on the radios (or maybe keyup?) to update attributes when the checked state changes.

Therefore fix: In Radios.prototype.init, after setting up click listener, also add event listener for 'change' on the $module (or on each input) to call this.handleChange (similar to handleClick) which will update attributes for all radios with aria-controls.

Alternatively, we could modify handleClick to also be called on change. Since the behavior for click and change should be same: when a radio with aria-controls changes its checked state, we need to update aria-expanded and toggle conditional.

